{"name":"Osiris","hex":"#5b5a4d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Osiris","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The Egyptian god of the dead and of the underworld.","An exoplanet in the Pegasus constellation, HD 209458 b."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Osiris"}],"entries":[{"title":"Osiris","blocks":[{"text":"Osiris was the god of fertility, agriculture, the afterlife, the dead, resurrection, life, and vegetation in ancient Egyptian religion. He was classically depicted with a pharaoh's beard, partially mummy-wrapped at the legs, wearing a distinctive atef crown and holding a symbolic crook and flail. He was one of the first to be associated with the mummy wrap. When his brother Set cut him to pieces after killing him, Osiris's sister-wife, Isis, with her sister Nephthys, searched Egypt to find each part of him. She collected all but one – Osiris's genitalia. She then wrapped his body up, enabling him to return to life. Osiris was widely worshipped until the decline of ancient Egyptian religion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Standing_Osiris.svg/330px-Standing_Osiris.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osiris","wikidata":"Q46491"},{"title":"Osiris (DC Comics)","blocks":[{"text":"Osiris is a fictional character in American comic books published by DC Comics. First appearing in 52 #23, the character was created by Geoff Johns, Greg Rucka, and Grant Morrison. Throughout the publication of the character's history, he often serves as both an analogue to Captain Marvel Jr./Shazam Jr. in Black Adam's supporting cast while being depicted as a superhero, antihero, and villain."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1f/Osiris_%28Amon_Tomaz%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osiris_(DC_Comics)","wikidata":"Q3196749"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Osiris_-_God.png/500px-Osiris_-_God.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Osiris_-_God.png/1280px-Osiris_-_God.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Osiris_-_God.png/500px-Osiris_-_God.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Osiris was sometimes depicted with black skin, symbolizing the underworld deities and fertility of the Nile floodplain.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOsiris_-_God.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Head_of_the_God_Osiris%2C_ca._595-525_B.C.E..jpg/500px-Head_of_the_God_Osiris%2C_ca._595-525_B.C.E..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Head_of_the_God_Osiris%2C_ca._595-525_B.C.E..jpg/500px-Head_of_the_God_Osiris%2C_ca._595-525_B.C.E..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Head of the God Osiris, c. 595–525 BC. Brooklyn Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHead_of_the_God_Osiris%2C_ca._595-525_B.C.E..jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Tumba_de_Tutankam%C3%B3n%2C_Valle_de_las_Reyes%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_75.jpg/500px-Tumba_de_Tutankam%C3%B3n%2C_Valle_de_las_Reyes%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_75.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Tumba_de_Tutankam%C3%B3n%2C_Valle_de_las_Reyes%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_75.jpg/1280px-Tumba_de_Tutankam%C3%B3n%2C_Valle_de_las_Reyes%2C_Luxor%2C_Egipto%2C_2022-04-03%2C_DD_75.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or